<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Sanjō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1034">1034</a>–<a href="/years/1073">1073</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Go-Sanjō-tennō, Takahito, Sanjō II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Go-Sanjō was the 71st emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> according to the traditional order of succession, reigning from 1068 to 1073. A son of Emperor Go-Suzaku and Princess Sadako, he was the first emperor in 170 years whose mother was not of Fujiwara descent paternally, so he owed the Fujiwara regents no special loyalty and was determined to rule personally. In 1069 he issued the Enkyū <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoen">Shōen</a> Regulation Decree and set up an office to certify the records of the shōen estates. It was during and due to his reign that the Fujiwara grip on power was broken. He abdicated in favour of his son Shirakawa in 1073, became a monk and died the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-sanjo.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Sanjō" width="330" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 石本秋園 (Ishimoto Shūen) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Go-sanjō.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An emperor born of another mother could slip their hold: Emperor Uda regained much of the government, and <b>Emperor Go-Sanjō</b>, from 1068, curbed the Fujiwara and began the rule of retired emperors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
